# Guardian AI — Project Rules

Guardian AI is a **focused technical-challenge prototype**: a functional demo of an
agentic AI code-review system, not a production platform. Small, explainable scope beats
breadth or polish.

- Before implementing any significant change, read the relevant parts of
  `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`, `docs/DECISIONS.md`, and `docs/ROADMAP.md`. Do not guess at
  architecture that is already documented.
- Preserve the documented scope. Do not expand capabilities, add endpoints/screens, or
  add tools beyond what `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` and `docs/ROADMAP.md` describe.
- Prefer the simplest implementation that is still explainable in a short presentation
  over a more "impressive" but harder-to-justify one.
- Never introduce undocumented technologies or features. In particular, never add:
  Redis, Kubernetes, a microservices split, RAG, vector databases, PyTorch or model
  training, authentication/authorization, CI/CD pipeline integration, or an asynchronous
  job queue. These are explicit non-goals.
- Keep all provider-specific AI logic (auth, request/response shape, prompt formatting)
  strictly inside provider adapters under `providers/`. Orchestrator and Decision Agent
  code must depend only on the `AIProvider` interface.
- Keep deterministic evidence (Ruff, Pytest, ESLint, etc.) and semantic evidence (LLM)
  clearly separate in code and data, merging only through the common `Evidence` shape.
  The system must never rely on the LLM alone.
- Do not modify files unrelated to the current task. Keep diffs scoped to what was asked.
- If an architectural deviation seems necessary, explain the reasoning and trade-off
  before implementing it — do not silently diverge from the documented design.
- Use English for all code, identifiers, comments, commit messages, and technical
  documentation.
- Update `docs/TASKS.md` only when a task is genuinely completed and verified — do not
  check off tasks speculatively or in advance.
